            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest Perseverance rover news is that NASA/JPL reported on Aug. 5, 2026 that Perseverance captured a first-of-its-kind view from the Martian surface: Earth disappearing behind Mars’ moon Phobos. The sequence was taken by the rover’s Mastcam-Z camera on July 2, 2026, during sol 1,907 of the mission; Earth appears as a tiny bright dot and briefly “winks out” behind Phobos in the Martian evening sky 【5:1†source】.\n\nOther recent Perseverance updates include:\n\n- **Ancient impact record at “Broom Point”**: NASA said Perseverance found evidence that a 245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick stack of rocks on Jezero Crater’s rim was formed by repeated asteroid impacts, helping scientists reconstruct ancient Martian geology 【5:2†source】.\n- **A “marathon” milestone**: The rover recently completed 26.2 miles / 42.195 km of driving on Mars, reaching that distance on sol 1,890 after about five years and four months of surface operations 【5:4†source】.\n- **Ongoing imagery**: NASA’s Perseverance raw image archive continues to publish recent rover images, including an “Image of the Week” for July 26–Aug. 1, 2026 【5:0†source】.",
                "annotations": [
                  {
                    "type": "url_citation",
                    "url": "https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasas-perseverance-rover-watches-earth-vanish-behind-martian-moon/",
